CONFIGURATION
AP SSID: CS640-A4-10; AP IP: 192.168.4.1/24; client IP: 192.168.4.2.
Transport: custom 20-byte header over raw IPv4 protocol 253, no TCP or UDP.
Receive queue: eight slots, destination port 6401; application delay: zero.
20 messages; payloads 1–19: 1460 bytes; payload 20: 17 bytes.
Initial sender delay: 2000 ms configured; interval: 100 ms configured.
No custom transport ACKs, retransmissions, or injected faults in this checkpoint.

Q1(a) DECODE AN ACTUAL HEADER AND VERIFY ITS CRC
AP timestamp 76509 ms, sequence 1:
19 00 19 01 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 05 b4 00 00 a9 76 95 ad

Byte offsets   Hex bytes        Field                 Value
0–1            19 00            Source port           6400
2–3            19 01            Destination port      6401
4–7            00 00 00 01      Sequence number       1
8–11           00 00 00 00      ACK number            0
12–13          05 b4            Payload length        1460
14–15          00 00            Window                0
16–19          a9 76 95 ad      CRC-32                0xA97695AD

Fields are big-endian. The AP reports an IPv4 header length of 20 bytes,
which it skips before parsing this transport header. It zeroes the CRC field
and computes CRC-32/IEEE over the 20-byte transport header and actual payload,
excluding the IPv4 header. Received CRC and recomputed CRC are both A97695AD:
PASS. The startup CRC self-test also reports 123456789 -> CBF43926 PASS.

Q1(b) VERIFY THE FINAL SHORT PAYLOAD
AP timestamp 78423 ms decodes sequence 20 with length 17 and matching received
and recomputed CRC 908243EE. At 78454 ms it logs:
APP delivery number=20 expected=20 length=17 wait_ms=79 Q=0 verify=PASS
APP SUMMARY messages=20 bytes=27757 order/length/every-byte=PASS

The application verified the expected message numbers, order, lengths and every
payload byte. The total is 19 * 1460 + 17 = 27757 application bytes. The final
transport packet is only 20 + 17 = 37 bytes, as the transmitter also records;
it is not padded to a full-size payload.

Q1(c) DELAY THE FIRST SEND AND SHOW BLOCKING DELIVERY
Transmitter-local observations:
- 5639 ms: first-send delay announced as 2000 ms.
- 7650 ms: DATA 1 send succeeds.
- Observed interval: 7650 - 5639 = 2011 ms (includes logging/scheduling overhead).
- Subsequent send records are 101 ms apart, approximately the configured 100 ms.
- All 20 sends return the requested byte count. This alone does not prove receipt.

AP-local observations, recovered with their original timestamps in the replay:
- 1246 ms: APP WAIT, expected=1.
- 76529 ms: transport enqueues DATA 1; application records delivery_ms=76529.
- 76540 ms: application prints verification PASS for message 1.
- Recorded wait-to-delivery interval: 76529 - 1246 = 75283 ms.

The AP interval includes time before the sender starts and is not a measurement
of the sender's two-second delay. Its exact pre-send components are unmeasured.
The timestamp interval also includes surrounding logging/scheduling overhead,
so it is not an isolated measurement of CPU time spent inside the queue call.
Do not subtract AP timestamps from transmitter timestamps.

Blocking call: src/main.cpp:74, applicationTask:
if (xQueueReceive(messages, &message, portMAX_DELAY) != pdTRUE) continue;

With an empty queue, portMAX_DELAY makes the application task block until a
message arrives rather than repeatedly polling. The separate transport task
validates a packet and calls xQueueSend; the application wakes and checks the
payload. The AP WAIT, enqueue, timing, and delivery records demonstrate this path.

EVIDENCE LIMITS
The supplied captures support the observations above. Same-transfer pairing is
not established merely by the reused run ID. Keep this qualification if writing
up these captures together. The required photo of both boards is a separate
report artifact and is not supplied by a serial log.

